The three fates, or moirè, aren't past, present and future, but the
spinsters. You must be confusing them with the ghosts past, present and
future from Dickens' "Christmas Carol".

About the moirè:

Moirè: with the ancient Greeks the "goddesses of fate". The word "moira"
means "part", or "portion". Homer uses it (in singular) to denote the
total of all thing shat happen to a numan being. On the other side, Homer
also tells us that the gods "spin" good or evil towards someone.

On one place in Homer (Odysseia VII 197) this is done by the Klothes (the
spinsters). But only in the Illiad XXIV 49 does Homer call the Moirè
goddesses.

Since Hesiod the 3 Moirè are known, often depicted as the spinsters of
fate and life. Hesoid calls them the daughters of Zeus and Themis; they
are called Klotho (the spinster), Lachesis (she who deals out), and
Atropos (the inevitable). The first makes the thread of life, the second
guides it, when the third cuts it, man dies.

